<li>Illinois</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'IA')}">
<li>Iowa</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'KY')}">
<li>Kentucky</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'ME')}">
<li>Maine</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'MA')}">
<li>Massachusetts</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'MS')}">
<li>Mississippi</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'NV')}">
<li>Nevada</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'ND')}">
<li>North Dakota</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'OH')}">
<li>Ohio</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'RI')}">
<li>Rhode Island</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'SD')}">
<li>South Dakota</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
<apex:outputPanel rendered="{!CONTAINS(relatedTo.Employees_work_in_the_following_states__c ,'VT')}">
<li>Vermont</li>
</apex:outputPanel>
</ul>
</apex:outputPanel>
<p><b>State Employment Tax Accounts Information</b></p>

<p>Closure of a client’s state payroll tax account is solely a client decision. As a client of <br/>
Insperity, you may choose to leave your employment tax accounts open. If you do leave <br/>
your accounts open, you are required to timely file all reports for as long as the accounts <br/>
are active – even if they are zero reports.</p>

<p>For more information visit the Closing State Employment Tax Accounts section on the <br/>
<apex:outputLink value="https://portal.insperity.com/pcms/payroll_tax_guidelines" target="_parent"> Payroll Tax Guidelines page</apex:outputLink> on Insperity Premier.</p>

<p><b>Regular Rate of Pay Resources</b></p>

<p>At Insperity, one of our main goals is to provide clients with the necessary information to <br/>
make informed decisions regarding compliance. Most companies are aware of the portion of the <br/>
Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA), that addresses exempt/non-exempt employees and the overtime <br/>
requirements associated with these employees. It has been my experience that few businesses <br/>
understand their responsibility or how to calculate RRP for non-exempt employees. Regular rate <br/>
of pay calculation may be required if an employee classified as hourly or salary non-exempt, <br/>
who works overtime and receives additional compensation during the same pay period.</p>

<p>I have attached the following information for your review:</p>

<p>* A Fact Sheet from the Department of Labor (DOL) website explaining Regular Rate of Pay</p>

<p>Please review this information and if you have questions after reviewing the information provided, <br/>
please do not hesitate to reach out to me.</p>

<p>Please note: As stated in the Client Service Agreement (CSA), compliance with the FLSA and any similar <br/>
state law is the client’s responsibility. By providing the information and suggestions contained in <br/>
this communication, Insperity is not assuming any liability or responsibility for FLSA compliance and <br/>
is not intending to amend or alter in any way the terms of the CSA. The suggestions and feedback we <br/>
provide are based on the information that you, the client, provide to us such as current job titles and <br/>
duties of the employees working at your business as well as the payroll information you submit. <br/>
Insperity does not warrant or guaranty the accuracy of the information or suggestions contained herein. <br/>
We recommend that you periodically (at least once a year) review your classifications to confirm that <br/>
they remain appropriate, especially concerning exempt positions. FLSA classification can be a difficult <br/> 
and inexact process. You may wish to consult with legal counsel experienced in wage and hour law to assist <br/>
you in fulfilling your responsibilities under the FLSA and CSA.</p>

<p>Please let me know if you have any questions.</p>

<p>Regards,<br/>
{!relatedTo.NSP_Payroll_Specialist__r.Name}<br/>
{!relatedTo.NSP_Payroll_Specialist__r.NSP_Job_Title__c}<br/>
{!relatedTo.NSP_Payroll_Specialist__r.NSP_Work_email__c}</p>
 <c:InsperityEmailFooter ></c:InsperityEmailFooter> 
 </body>
</html>
</messaging:HtmlEmailBody>
</messaging:emailTemplate>